================================================================================
TEST T44987 -- ACL: Hardware IP 3000 range, applied to ports
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09, box local time (device clocks were set to box local time
          during setup, so device and host stamps are comparable)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2, u3, u5; Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWP-8612, objective "a numbered hardware IP access-list in the 3000
          range is configurable for each of its parameters and takes effect when
          applied to a port with access-group", 8 steps as run
Build:    swi_c  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (x960-tb277.rel, netboots; boot config
                 flash:/default.cfg)
          swi_b  sin_x96056-20260921-9 (AT-x960-28YSQ, console u3)
Configs:  swi_c.cfg, swi_b.cfg (running-config after setup; saved to startup: yes,
          once, for the reload in STEP 7)

VERDICT: PASS -- numbered hardware IP ACLs in the 3000 range bind to a port with
         `access-group` and filter correctly in all five protocol forms, with the
         configuration and its enforcement surviving a reload.
      3000-range list accepted ........ yes        (stored as "Hardware IP access list 3000")
      bound to a port ................. works      (access-group 3000 in running-config)
      matching traffic ................ denied     (100% packet loss; Packet Hits: 4 = 4 pings)
      non-matching source ............. permitted  (0% loss on the identical path)
      five protocol forms ............. accepted   (icmp, ip, proto, tcp, udp)
      protocol selectivity ............ correct    (tcp and udp rules leave ICMP alone)
      permit rules .................... work       (counter +4 for 4 pings, not implicit permit)
      non-IP (ARP) traffic ............ unaffected (re-resolved while ICMP was denied)
      several access-groups on a port . accepted   (five lists bound at once)
      classifier cost ................. 1 per bound rule (2/1024 -> 7/1024 for five)
      unbound lists ................... cost nothing (six defined, one bound, ACL = 1)
      editing a bound list ............ refused    (platform constraint, STEP 3)
      persistence across a reload ..... holds      (definitions, binding and enforcement)
CAVEAT:  The filtering was measured with ICMP only, ingressing a VLAN 100 access port on a
         standalone DUT. The tcp and udp forms were therefore proven only negatively -- they
         correctly did NOT match ICMP; no TCP or UDP flow was offered to them, so their
         positive match was not demonstrated. Port numbers, TCP flags and the remaining
         3000-range qualifiers were not exercised, nor was any egress direction: `access-group`
         is an ingress filter and only the ingress path was measured. The persistence test was
         a software `reload` after `copy running-config startup-config`, not a power cycle.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   tb277 eth4   00:0d:b9:55:77:0f
     10.38.22.65/27  = the MATCHING source
     10.38.22.66/27  = the NON-MATCHING control source
        |
        v   ICMP echo request, VLAN 100 -- ingresses the DUT here
   port1.0.48   <== ACL BINDING POINT: access-group 3000 / 3001 / 3002 / 3003 /
                    3004 / 3005 (an INGRESS filter; this is the measured point)
  +==========================================================+
  |   swi_c   AT-x960-56YSQ   *** DUT ***   Marvell 98CX8514 |
  |                                         Falcon           |
  +==========================================================+
        |  sa1 = port1.0.2 + port1.0.16
        v  forwarded on toward the far side (VLAN 100)
   swi_b sa1
  +----------------------------------+
  |  swi_b   AT-x960-28YSQ           |
  |  vlan100  10.38.22.70/27         |   far side: "was it forwarded, or dropped?"
  +----------------------------------+

  Why port1.0.48 is the binding point: `access-group` is an INGRESS filter, and host->swi_b
  traffic ingresses the DUT on port1.0.48, so a rule written src=host dst=swi_b matches
  directly there.

  DUT     : swi_c (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 @115200, S/N A2WQS7004,
            bridge MAC e01a.ead4.72cb, Marvell 98CX8514 Falcon, netboots
  far side: swi_b (AT-x960-28YSQ) console u3 @9600, S/N A10737A261700021,
            bridge MAC 0000.cd42.3029, STP root
  link    : swi_c port1.0.48 <-> tb277 eth4 (VLAN 100 access)
  link    : swi_c sa1 (port1.0.2 + port1.0.16) <-> swi_b sa1 (VLAN 100)
  host    : tb277 eth4 00:0d:b9:55:77:0f, 10.38.22.65/27 and 10.38.22.66/27
            -> swi_c port1.0.48
  unused  : swi_a (console u5) was deliberately isolated and carried no traffic in this case
  tools   : ping and ip(8) on tb277; console driving with tools/ckcon.py, tools/ckyn.py,
            tools/ckreload.py and tools/cfg.py on tb277

STEP 0 -- baseline (no ACL exists)                                        done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
x960-56YSQ#show class-map
x960-56YSQ#show policy-map
                                            <-- all empty
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  Loop Detection      2
  Global ACL          0
  ACL                 0
  VACL                0
  QoS                 0
  Total               2 / 1024 (0.20%)      <-- TCAM baseline
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 -> 0% packet loss
                                            <-- both sources reach the far side before any
                                                ACL exists, so a later loss is attributable
                                                to the ACL and not to the path

STEP 1 -- "a 3000-range list applied to a port denies the matching flow"   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3000 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 3000
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3000
      4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
  interface port1.0.48
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   switchport access vlan 100
   access-group 3000                        <-- the binding is in the running config
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss      PERMITTED  <-- the control
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters
  Name: 3000
      Rule: deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      Packet Hits: 4                        <-- exactly the 4 denied pings
  => STEP 1 PASS: a numbered hardware IP ACL bound to a port denies the traffic its rule
     matches and leaves an identical flow from another source untouched, with the hit counter
     corroborating the packet count. The two sources differ only in address, so the denial
     follows the rule rather than the path.

STEP 2 -- "non-IP traffic is unaffected"                                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: an IP hardware ACL should filter IP only. ARP is forced to re-resolve across the
  same path while the ICMP flow from the same host is denied.
host# ip neigh flush dev eth4 ; ping .70 from 10.38.22.65    (denied)
host# ip neigh show dev eth4
  10.38.22.70 lladdr 00:00:cd:42:30:29 REACHABLE   <-- ARP completed through the DUT
  => STEP 2 PASS: ARP re-resolved across the path while ICMP from the same host was denied,
     so an IP hardware ACL does not filter non-IP frames.

STEP 3 -- "Configure hardware IP 3000 range ACL for each of the parameters"
          -- a bound numbered ACL cannot be edited                        done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  This platform constraint is not anticipated by the step text, and it shapes how the
  remaining parameters had to be configured (one list per rule, STEP 4).
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3000 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  % This access-list is currently attached to an interface so cannot be edited.
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3000
      4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32    <-- unchanged; the entry was NOT added
  Also measured, the bare list with no action:
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3000
  % Incomplete command.
  => STEP 3 done: a numbered hardware ACL is read-only while an `access-group` binds it. It
     must be unbound before it can be edited, or a separate list used per rule. The refusal
     is silent in its effect -- the entry is simply not added -- so `show access-list` has to
     be re-read to notice.

STEP 4 -- "attach each access-list to different ports" -- all five protocol
          forms, one list each, and their SELECTIVITY                     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group 3000
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3001 deny ip    10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3002 deny proto 1 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3003 deny tcp   10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3004 deny udp   10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3000     4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  Hardware IP access list 3001     4 deny ip   10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  Hardware IP access list 3002     4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32   <-- see note
  Hardware IP access list 3003     4 deny tcp  10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  Hardware IP access list 3004     4 deny udp  10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32

  NOTE: `deny proto 1` was CANONICALISED to `deny icmp` in list 3002. Protocol 1 is ICMP, so
  the CLI stores and displays the named form. Worth knowing when diffing a running config
  against what was typed.

  Each list was then bound to port1.0.48 in turn, with ICMP offered from both sources:

    list  stored rule   .65 (ICMP)          .66 (ICMP)       expected
    3000  deny icmp     100% packet loss    0% packet loss   DENY  (icmp matches)
    3001  deny ip       100% packet loss    0% packet loss   DENY  (ip matches all IP)
    3002  deny icmp     100% packet loss    0% packet loss   DENY  (proto 1 = icmp)
    3003  deny tcp        0% packet loss    0% packet loss   PASS  (tcp does not match icmp)
    3004  deny udp        0% packet loss    0% packet loss   PASS  (udp does not match icmp)

  => STEP 4 PASS: all five protocol forms are accepted, bind to a port and behave correctly.
     The last two rows are the important ones: a TCP rule and a UDP rule both leave ICMP
     alone, so the protocol field genuinely selects rather than every rule matching
     everything. The three DENY rows and the two PASS rows are each other's controls, and
     the .66 column is the per-row control on the same cabling.

STEP 5 -- "permit statements work" -- proven by the counter               PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: hardware ACLs implicitly PERMIT what no rule denies, so "the traffic passed" is NOT
  proof that a permit rule worked. The rule's own counter is.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3005 permit icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group 3005
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 3005
      Rule: permit icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      Packet Hits: 3
host# ping -c 4 .70 from 10.38.22.65
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 3005
      Packet Hits: 7                        <-- +4 for 4 pings
  => STEP 5 PASS: the permit rule's counter advances exactly with the traffic offered, so the
     rule is being matched in hardware and is not merely being shadowed by the implicit
     permit.

