     `access-group` lines at once.

STEP 7 -- persistence across a save and reload                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
x960-56YSQ#show boot
  Current software   : x960-tb277.rel
  Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg (file exists)
  Config under test: all groups removed except `access-group 3000` on port1.0.48.
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      (denied before the reload)
x960-56YSQ#copy running-config startup-config
  Building configuration... [OK]
x960-56YSQ#reload
  reboot system? (y/n): y
                                            <-- login seen again after 119 s

  AFTER THE RELOAD:
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  all six lists 3000-3005 present, unchanged                  <-- definitions SURVIVED
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
  interface port1.0.48
   access-group 3000                                          <-- binding SURVIVED
x960-56YSQ#show static-channel-group
  Member: port1.0.2, port1.0.16                               <-- aggregation SURVIVED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss           STILL DENIED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss           STILL PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ#show platform classifier statistics utilization brief
  Loop Detection      2
  ACL                 1
  Total               3 / 1024 (0.29%)
  => STEP 7 PASS: the ACL definitions, the port binding and the enforcement all survived a
     save and reload, with the non-matching control still passing afterwards.
     Also proven here: six lists are defined but only ONE is bound, and the classifier reports
     ACL = 1. An UNBOUND access-list consumes no classifier entry -- the rule budget is spent
     by BINDINGS, not by definitions, so a configuration can hold far more rules than the
     1024-entry pool as long as most are unbound.
